{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 21, :comments ["<b>You shall not plant…, and you shall not set up…, you shall not slaughter….</b> In the Midrash they said (Deuteronomy Rabbah 5:8): on the throne of King Solomon there were six steps, and written on the first was 'לֹא תַטֶּה' ('you shall not pervert'), on the second 'לֹא תַכִּיר' ('you shall not show partiality'), on the third 'לֹא תִקַּח' ('you shall not take'), on the fourth 'לֹא תִטַּע' ('you shall not plant'), on the fifth 'לֹא תָקִים' ('you shall not set up'), on the sixth 'לֹא תִזְבַּח' ('you shall not slaughter'). And every discerning person will be astonished at the sight: what do 'לֹא תִזְבַּח' and 'לֹא תָקִים' and 'לֹא תִטַּע' have to do with the throne of Solomon which was prepared for justice? And the dictum 'לֹא תִזְבַּח' — which may seem far-removed in your eyes to be attached to the throne of judgment — behold, I shall show a place where the matter is very close, in the manner that they said (Yevamot 101a): just as the court had to be clean in justice, so too they had to be clean of blemish, as it says (Song of Songs 4:7), 'כֻּלָּךְ יָפָה רַעְיָתִי וּמוּם אֵין בָּךְ' ('You are all fair, my love, and there is no blemish in you'). And it is known that there is nothing that comes by tradition that has no support from the Torah, for we do not derive matters of Torah from matters of tradition (Chagigah 10a). And from where did they learn to say thus from the Torah — that the Sanhedrin must be clean of every blemish? Rather, surely because everyone agrees: even one who does not derive adjacencies from every place in Mishneh Torah, everyone does derive adjacencies (Yevamot 4b). If so, what emerges from the fact that these six are juxtaposed to these prohibitions? Only to tell you that all of them depend on the one who sits on the throne of judgment. And it was not for nothing that the Torah said that the place of the Sanhedrin's session should be by the altar — rather, surely in order to learn this from that: everything that disqualifies in the altar itself and in the offerings brought upon it, that is the very thing that disqualifies also in the Sanhedrin." "Regarding the offerings upon the altar it is written: 'לֹא תִזְבַּח לַה׳ אֱלֹהֶיךָ שׁוֹר אוֹ שֶׂה אֲשֶׁר יִהְיֶה בוֹ מוּם כֹּל דָּבָר רָע' ('You shall not slaughter to YHWH your Elohim an ox or a sheep in which there is a blemish, any evil thing') — just as a blemish disqualifies in the offering, so too it disqualifies in the Sanhedrin. For every external blemish reveals some hidden evil within the body, whether in man or in beast. Therefore regarding an animal it says: 'כֹּל דָּבָר רָע' ('any evil thing'), for the revealed blemish indicates that within it there is some hidden evil, some illness or wound. And the Torah wrote this for us in order to reveal the reason for the blemish that disqualifies also in the Sanhedrin — for the external blemish proceeds from the root that sprouts 'פֹּרֶה רֹאשׁ וְלַעֲנָה' ('wormwood and gall'), in the manner we explained above in parashat Emor (21:21) on the verse 'מוּם בּוֹ' ('a blemish in him') — see there. By way of example: if he is blind in one eye, we know of him that bribery caused it; and if he is lame, we know that his leg did not stand straight and he limps and hobbles on both sides; and so too all blemishes. And this is what they said: 'just as they must be clean in justice' etc. — why is the blemish tied to justice? Rather, surely because this depends on that: the blemish indicates a corruption of justice. Therefore Scripture juxtaposed the verse 'לֹא תִזְבַּח' to the verse 'צֶדֶק צֶדֶק תִּרְדֹּף' ('Justice, justice shall you pursue')." "And just as the altar needed to be of many stones and not a single pillar (matzevet even), so too the Sanhedrin — for one judges alone only One (Avot 4:10). Therefore the Sanhedrin needed to be many, for men of excellence are likened to 'אַבְנֵי נֵזֶר' (crown-stones) in many places in Scripture. Accordingly, it was written on Solomon's throne 'לֹא תָקִים לְךָ מַצֵּבָה' ('You shall not set up a pillar for yourself'). And 'לֹא תִטַּע לְךָ אֲשֵׁרָה' ('You shall not plant an Asherah for yourself') — they interpreted (Sanhedrin 7b): anyone who appoints an unfit judge is as if he planted an Asherah, and if it is in the place of Torah scholars, it is as if he planted it next to the altar of YHWH. And this is a great warning against arrogance, and we learn this too from the altar: that YHWH did not desire an altar of silver and gold but rather 'מִזְבַּח אֲדָמָה תַּעֲשֶׂה לִי' ('an altar of earth you shall make for Me'), which points to the quality of humility. And our Rabbis of blessed memory said (Avot 4:9): one whose heart is arrogant in halakhic ruling is a fool, wicked, and arrogant of spirit. As it is written (Psalms 25:9): 'יַדְרֵךְ עֲנָוִים בַּמִּשְׁפָּט' ('He guides the humble in justice'). And regarding the reason for this matter, we elaborated above in parashat Mishpatim, according to the Midrash brought in the Yalkut on that parashah, on the verse 'שֹׁפְטִים וְשֹׁטְרִים': a parable of a king who had a beautiful orchard and he had a small son etc., and there we offered a precious commentary on this Midrash that substantiates this matter — that arrogance corrupts all judgments. And the root of the corruption sprouts from those judges who are appointed for the sake of praise and favoritism — from the haughtiness of his own heart or the heart of his relatives — who pursue the excess of honor and authority and plant this Asherah so that he may be powerful on earth, as the word 'Asherah' suggests. And our Rabbis of blessed memory said (Sotah 5a): anyone in whom there is arrogance is fit to be cut down like an Asherah etc. — hear from this that one law applies to the Asherah and to those who shelter under its shade: both are cut down and uprooted. 'And in the place of Torah scholars as if he planted it next to the altar' — for there is the seat of the Sanhedrin, that is, 'in the place of Torah scholars.'" "And in our generation — how many Asherot they plant in every city! They do not investigate whether it is the place of a Torah scholar or not. Even when there is in his city a Torah scholar outstanding in Torah learning and piety, all his wisdom avails him nothing, for in any event each person chooses his relative or his in-law, whom he knows will flatter him or will oppose his enemy. And this breach is at its full among the multitudes of the people of my nation, for the sake of the seed of Abraham it is time to act. And in any event, it has come to my hand to offer a precious commentary on the juxtaposition of these six prohibitions."], :book :deuteronomy, :voice :kli-yakar, :lang :en, :chapter 16, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T13:13:02.543125084Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}